# Forms

Forms use the field partials (`input`, `label`, `textarea`, `select`,
`checkbox`, `radio_group`, `switch`, `combobox`) composed by the page.
Markup is native form elements styled by basecoat; submission is a
normal Rails form with Turbo — no client-side form framework.

## Choosing a control

| The user needs to… | Use |
| ------------------- | --- |
| Enter short free text | `input` |
| Enter long free text | `textarea` |
| Pick one of ≤5 known options | `radio_group` — all options visible |
| Pick one of many options | `select` |
| Pick one from a long or unfamiliar list | `combobox` — searchable |
| Pick several from a list | one `checkbox` per option |
| Turn something on or off, applied with the form | `checkbox` |
| Turn something on or off, applied immediately | `switch` |
| Find a patient | `patient_search` composite |

Two controls to avoid misusing: a `select` with two options should be
a `radio_group` (both visible beats hidden behind a click); a `switch`
inside a form that still needs a submit button should be a `checkbox`
(a switch implies the change already happened).

## Field layout

- Single column, labels above fields, `gap-4` between fields. Pair
  fields side by side only for genuinely linked values (date ranges).
- Group related fields into sections with a section heading; long
  forms become cards per section rather than one wall of fields.
- Mark the exception, not the rule: in a mostly-required form, mark
  optional fields "(optional)" in the label; never asterisk every
  field.
- Help text sits under the field in `text-sm text-muted-foreground`,
  present before the user gets it wrong — format examples belong in
  placeholders, rules belong in help text.

## Validation and errors

- Validation is server-side; the form re-renders with errors. Do not
  duplicate rules in JavaScript — browser-native `required` and
  `type=` attributes are the only client-side layer.
- On error: a summary alert at the top of the form states that saving
  failed, and each failing field shows its message below the field in
  `text-critical`. Field messages name the rule (see the *Content*
  foundations page).
- Keep the user's input on re-render. Never clear a failed form.

## Actions

- One primary button per form, labelled with the outcome ("Save
  referral"), placed first in the action row, with "Cancel" as a
  ghost button beside it.
- Disable-and-spin on submit via the button's `loading` param —
  double submission is prevented at the button, not with alerts.
- Destructive actions never sit directly beside the primary action:
  separate them visually and confirm via dialog.
